---
// Matches what the app does at the published version (PreCut 6b2636a): its cloud AI texts
// (Settings → Cloud AI → What is sent), docs/store/privacy-policy.md and docs/CLOUD_API.md.
import ProseLayout from "../layouts/ProseLayout.astro";
import { app, contactEmail, legalName, legalUpdated } from "../config";
---

<ProseLayout
  title="Privacy policy"
  description={`What the ${app.name} app and this website do with your information.`}
  updated={legalUpdated}
>
  <p>
    {app.name} is a video editor for Android, made by {legalName}. This policy
    explains what the {app.name} app and this website do with your information.
  </p>
  <p class="card">
    <strong>In short:</strong> {app.name} edits on your phone, and nothing
    leaves it unless you use a cloud AI feature and agree, that time, to send
    the sound. Then only the sound is sent, to turn speech into words. There are
    no accounts, no ads and no analytics.
  </p>

  <h2>Cloud AI (optional, and asked every time)</h2>
  <p>
    Some features can use cloud AI: today{" "}
    <strong>Settings → Cloud AI → Check cloud AI</strong>, and automatic
    captions when they arrive. Before anything is sent, {app.name} shows what it
    is about to send and asks you. If you say no, nothing is sent. You can
    switch cloud AI off in <strong>Settings → Cloud AI</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>When you agree:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>What is sent:</strong> only the sound you're making captions for
      (for Check cloud AI, a test phrase your phone speaks). Never the video,
      photos or project.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Where it goes:</strong>
      {app.name}'s server, which runs on{" "}
      <a href="https://supabase.com/privacy">Supabase</a>
      in Ireland (EU). It passes the sound to ElevenLabs, a speech-to-text
      service, and sends the words and their timings back to your phone.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What {app.name}'s server keeps:</strong> no audio and no words. It
      keeps a random ID that the app made for this installation (not linked to
      your name, email or phone number) and how many minutes it used this month,
      so each phone gets its free minutes. Both are deleted after 13 months
      without use, or when you tap
      <strong>Settings → Cloud AI → Delete my cloud data</strong>. The server's
      logs record each request's time, type, size and outcome with a shortened
      form of that ID, never the sound or the words. Like any web service, the
      hosting provider's logs also record your IP address. Logs are kept briefly
      to run and protect the service.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>ElevenLabs</strong> turns the sound into words for {app.name},
      under its own
      <a href="https://elevenlabs.io/privacy-policy">privacy policy</a> and data
      terms.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Google Play Integrity:</strong> before sending, {app.name} asks
      Google Play to confirm that it's the genuine {app.name} on a genuine
      Android phone, so no one else can use
      {app.name}'s minutes. Google Play makes that check under Google's terms,
      with information about the app and the device; {app.name} receives only
      the result.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Cloud AI needs the internet. Without a connection, {app.name} tells you and
    sends nothing.
  </p>

  <h2>What stays on your phone</h2>
  <p>To edit, {app.name} works with files on your device:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Photos and videos you pick.</strong> You choose them with
      Android's picker, and
      {app.name} can read only those. If the picker can't keep access to a file,{" "}
      {app.name} saves a copy in its own storage so your project still works
      later.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Microphone.</strong>
      {app.name} asks for the microphone only when you record a voiceover. The
      recording is saved in the app's storage as part of your project.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your projects.</strong>
      {app.name} saves drafts, covers, preview copies and other working files in
      its own storage on your phone.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Exports.</strong> Videos, GIFs, audio and photos you export are
      saved to your phone's gallery or music folders, where other apps can see
      them like any other media.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Notifications.</strong>
      {app.name} shows a notification while an export runs, so you can follow or
      cancel it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Crash reports.</strong> If {app.name} closes unexpectedly, it
      saves a short report (the error, your phone model, Android and app
      versions, and {app.name}'s own recent log lines) in its own storage. It
      stays there unless you share it yourself from
      <strong>Settings → Crash reports</strong>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    {app.name} has no accounts, no ads and no analytics. When you share an
    export, Android's share sheet sends it to the app you choose. What happens
    next is up to that app.
  </p>

  <h2>Text to speech</h2>
  <p>
    If you use text to speech, {app.name} passes your text to the text-to-speech
    engine installed on your phone. That engine's own privacy policy applies to
    the text.
  </p>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The website is hosted by{" "}
      <a href="https://www.netlify.com/privacy/">Netlify</a>. Like any web host,
      Netlify's servers record technical details of each visit, such as your IP
      address, browser and the page you asked for, to deliver and protect the
      site.
    </li>
    <li>
      The app download comes from
      <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub</a>,
      which records downloads in the same way.
    </li>
    <li>
      The website sets no cookies and uses no analytics or trackers. The
      download page checks whether you're on Android, an iPhone or a computer to
      show the right instructions; that check happens in your browser and
      nothing is sent.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Deleting your data</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Deleting a project removes its files from the app.
      <strong>Settings → Clear preview cache</strong> frees working copies.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Settings → Cloud AI → Delete my cloud data</strong> deletes the
      random ID and minutes
      {app.name}'s server keeps for this phone.
    </li>
    <li>
      Uninstalling {app.name} removes everything it stored on your phone, except
      media you exported to your gallery. What {app.name}'s server keeps is then
      deleted after 13 months without use.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    More detail: <a href="/delete-data">Delete your data</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>
    {app.name} isn't directed at children. Cloud AI sends only sound that the
    person using the app chooses to send.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If this policy changes, the new version will be posted on this page with a
    new date.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions or requests about your information:{" "}
    <a href={`mailto:${contactEmail}`}>{contactEmail}</a>.
  </p>
</ProseLayout>
